name w3-06-bingo-1
timeout 520
time 1991-01-19T00:00:00Z
wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

# CAMPAIGN W3, SORTIE 6 OF 10 - PACKAGE Q FAILURE MODE 1: FUEL, AND THE ONE PLACE IT BECOMES A DECISION.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w3-desert-storm.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none in the ten. Its attribution run A4 is this file with ONE LINE DELETED
# (`set brief_bingo_lbs 6000` on both escorts); the numbers are in
# doc/campaigns/w3-desert-storm.md, State.
#
# THE ANCHOR: the first of Package Q's three failures was a TANKER failure - bad weather on the tanker
# tracks and tankers arriving early at the release point; FOUR FIGHTERS ABORTED and others nearly
# stalled [T4]. FlightBox has no tanker, no boom and no external tank (gap C5), so the anchor's cause is
# not expressible. WHAT IS EXPRESSIBLE, and this file exists to find out how much of it there is, is the
# EFFECT: a fuel state that turns into a decision.
#
# WHAT THE TREE ACTUALLY HAS, read out of the source rather than assumed (rule 7). `set brief_bingo_lbs`
# is a real briefed number: `FBPilot` posts it over the command bus to the UFC, `modules/f16/FBF16Ufc`
# clamps it at the aircraft's own 6 070 lb ceiling, and `systems/FBWarningSystem` raises `FBWarnBingo`
# when the airframe block's fuel falls to or below it. Exactly ONE line of pilot code reads that
# warning: `FBPilot::CanPressOn`, and it is consulted at exactly ONE transition - the recovery OUT OF a
# defensive break. So the question this file can ask is sharp and small: DOES A BRIEFED FUEL NUMBER TURN
# A DEFENSIVE BREAK INTO AN ABORT, and does anything else in the sortie change at all?
#
# THE WEATHER IS THE ANCHOR'S OWN AND IT IS A REAL ATMOSPHERE, not a mood: `wx fixture` is the committed
# GFS blob (2026-07-27 00Z analysis step), sampled over Iraq. It is the only half of "bad weather on the
# tanker track" this tree can fly, it is NOT the anchor's weather (gap: a fixture has no time axis), and
# it is declared rather than dressed up. What it does here is drift, and the drift is measured.
#
# THE ARENA is w3-03's, with the package ingressing from further west so that the fuel state has a
# profile to be spent on; every coordinate is [SET]. `--elev const`, 0 m datum, no terrain masking (C4).
# WHICH DEFENSIVE LAYER THIS FILE MEASURES: the FIGHTER layer. The surface layer is present (a P-18 that
# radiates and a ZSU-23-4 that cannot reach 5 000 m) and briefed to hold; O5's rule that two layers
# cannot be measured in one file holds in W3 too.
# NIGHT: 03:00 local, sun about -48 deg [DERIVED]; nothing emits light, so the night costs the visual
# channel and nothing else - w3-01's header carries the full statement.
#
# THE SUBSTITUTION AND ITS DIRECTION: the anchor's escort was the F-15C. `modules/air/`'s `f15c` row is
# `ACCEPTED` as a FLIGHT MODEL and its promotion gate measures nothing about weapons, so no W3 file uses
# a catalogue row as a combatant. The escorts here are F-16s, which makes Blue no weaker.
# The MiG-29 stands in for the MiG-23/MiG-25 that were about 45 of the 55 Iraqi fighters aloft on
# 19 January [T4]; that makes RED STRONGER than history, and a FlightBox result that still goes well for
# Blue says more than the record did, while one that goes badly says nothing about 1991.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: WITHOUT A TANKER, DOES THE PILOT'S BINGO RULE TURN A FUEL STATE INTO A
# DECISION - and how much of the profile is reachable when it does?
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint f5b0904ebe45f0b1), AND THE ANSWER IS NO - THE FUEL STATE IS NOT A DECISION,
# AND THE DEFECT THAT MAKES IT SO IS PINNED RATHER THAN GUESSED:
#   the warning fires  `warn_active` = 2 (`FBWarnBingo`, 1 << 1) from t = 4.1 s to the end of the run
#                      on BOTH escorts. 40 % of 12 954 lb = 5 182 lb against a briefed 6 000, so the
#                      condition `FuelLbs <= BingoEffectiveLbs` holds for all 5 200 telemetry rows
#   the machine ignores it   `eng_state` on the lead: search -> closing (40.0) -> attack (156.1) ->
#                      support (180.1) -> DEFEND (181.1) -> SEARCH (210.0). Against attribution run A4,
#                      which is this file with the two bingo lines deleted: THE SAME SEVEN TRANSITIONS
#                      AT THE SAME SEVEN TICKS, on both escorts, `eng_state` byte-identical
#   the whole price    7 of 184 telemetry columns move (`warn_active` plus six command-bus counters,
#                      because the extra briefed number is one more command at spawn) and NOTHING ELSE.
#                      Zero metres of flight path, zero shots, zero verdicts
#   the strikers       2 of 2 released (t = 172.5 / 187.8), both aim points DESTROYED, in both runs
# THE CAUSE, read out of `pilot/FBPilot.cpp` and confirmed by the trace: `CanPressOn` is the ONLY line
# in the pilot that reads the BINGO bit, and it is consulted at exactly one place -
# `EngState_ == Defend && TimeS_ - IntThreatLastS_ >= DefendHoldS`. That branch is PREEMPTED: the chain
# ends in `else if (EngState_ != Abort)`, which fires on the first tick after `defendDue` goes false,
# and `IntThreatLastS_` is refreshed on every tick that `mustDefend` holds - so the elapsed time is
# still one tick when the general branch takes the state away. Measured here at 28.9 s of Defend
# (181.1 -> 210.0) ending in `search`, with the bingo bit set the whole time.
# SO PACKAGE Q'S FIRST FAILURE MODE IS BLOCKED TWICE OVER: by `C5` (no tanker, no tank, no fuel-driven
# RTB) and, underneath it, by a fuel rule the state machine cannot reach.
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   events.log      `intercept ENG_STATE ... to=defend` on an escort, then the state it recovers into -
#                   `search` (it presses on) or `abort` (the fuel number decided). Beside it, the
#                   strikers' `pilot ATTACK_RELEASE mode=ccrp` lines
#   telemetry_q6esca.csv  eng_state, and `warn_active` for the BINGO bit; the fuel column across the run
#
# READING RULE: exit 3 = TIMEOUT is expected. THE MEASUREMENT IS THE PAIR (the escorts' `eng_state`
# trace, the strikers that reached release) read beside attribution run A4, which is this file with the
# bingo line deleted. If the two runs differ in NOTHING, the honest report is that `C5` is not the only
# thing missing - the fuel state is not a decision in this tree either, and that sentence is the result.

net kari6
  control q6nod
  link wire
  period 4.0
  hold 3
  wcs free
  member q6nod
  member q6flk autonomy hold

unit q6esca
  module f16
  team friendly
  flight es6 1
  spawn 33.22000 43.40000 6000 90.0 480
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 40
  set fcr_mode crm
  set fcr_range_nm 40
  set rwr on
  set datalink on
  set store 1 aim120
  set store 2 aim120
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 4 aim120
  set store 6 aim9
  set store 7 aim9
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set brief_bingo_lbs 6000
  set task intercept
  wp 33.22000 44.80000 6000 480
  objective kill team hostile
  objective survive

